---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const CORRESPONDENCES = [
  ['Ashi points, acupoints', 'China', 'Stuck perforators — the staples', 'Heine: ~80% of points at fascial perforations; Langevin: 80% on connective-tissue planes; Maurer: bundles at only a few points'],
  ['Sinew channels and their knots (結)', 'China', 'Attachment lines of the sheet, where folds pile up', 'Proposed; untested'],
  ['Cou li — the texture between skin and flesh', 'China', 'The superficial fascial plane', 'Proposed'],
  ['Propagated sensation along the channel', 'China', 'Conducted vasodilation climbing a perforator tree', 'Speeds overlap (cm/s); untested together'],
  ['The wind points — Fengchi, Fengfu, Yifeng, Fengmen', 'China', 'Roots of the posterior trees; gates of the sheet', 'Fengchi sits where the occipital artery and nerve come through the fascia'],
  ['The Jade Pillow (玉枕), third gate of the orbit', 'Daoist', 'The occipital gate and the ring at the base of the skull', 'Anatomical convergence is real; the link is proposed'],
  ['Channel-knots (rtsa mdud) untied by the winds', 'Tibet', 'Stuck states released by breath', 'Proposed'],
  ['Granthis — Brahma, Viṣṇu, Rudra', 'India', 'Knots along the midline, the last between the brows (the front root of the galea)', 'Proposed'],
  ['Kuṇḍalinī rising; kriyās', 'India', 'Release travelling up trees; the sheet re-forming', 'Documented as experience (Lindahl 2017); mechanism open'],
  ['Saṅkhāras as solidified sensations', 'Buddhist', 'Stored holds that dissolve under equanimous attention and breath', 'Proposed'],
  ['Cupping and gua sha', 'China', 'Tools for the sheet: lift and shear the superficial plane', 'Mechanically plausible'],
];
---
<Base title="Traditions" current="traditions" description="Maps of the inner body from Chinese medicine, Daoist alchemy, yoga and tantra, Tibetan tsa lung and Dzogchen, and Buddhist practice — read beside the anatomy of perforators and fascia.">
  <PageHead kicker="Traditions" title="Maps of the inner body" glyph="経">
    Nearly every tradition that mapped the inner body arrived at the same three claims: there are knots, breath is the tool, and
    untying them changes consciousness.
  </PageHead>

  <article class="trad">
    <section>
      <p class="eyebrow">A note on reading</p>
      <p>
        These maps were drawn from the inside, in their own vocabularies, over two thousand years. They are not anatomy, and this page
        does not claim they were secretly describing it. What the essay proposes is narrower: where the traditions and modern reports
        converge on a <em>location</em>, the location is often a real convergence in the anatomy — and a correspondence specific enough to
        be coded blind and tested. Correspondence found after the fact is the weakest kind of evidence. Each map will be drawn on the same
        body in the <a href={`${base}/atlas/`}>atlas</a> so they can be compared, point by point.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="china">
      <span class="t-glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">経絡</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">China · Huangdi Neijing and after</p>
      <h2>Points, channels, and the knots of the sinews</h2>
      <p>
        Chinese medicine keeps <strong>two families</strong> of technique, and — read beside the perforator hypothesis — two families of
        structure. The first are points. The tender <em>ashi</em> points, named by Sun Simiao for the patient’s cry, are treated by the rule
        “take the painful spot as the point”. The 361 classical acupoints lie along twelve primary channels and the two midline vessels, the
        Governor (Du) up the back and the Conception (Ren) down the front.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second is the sheet. The <em>Neijing</em> describes the <em>cou li</em> — the interstices between skin and flesh through which the
        defensive qi circulates and through which pathogenic “wind” enters. And it describes the <em>jing jin</em>, twelve sinew channels of
        muscle and connective tissue whose defining feature is that each one <em>knots</em> — 結, jié, to bind or tie — at bony prominences:
        ankle, knee, hip, shoulder, the occiput, the mastoid, the cheekbone. The Bladder sinew channel knots at the back of the head. Its
        prescribed treatment is heat at the painful spot: fire needling and warm needling.
      </p>
      <p>
        The points at the base of the skull carry the names of wind: <strong>Fengchi</strong> (GB 20, Wind Pool) in the hollow between trapezius
        and sternocleidomastoid; <strong>Fengfu</strong> (GV 16, Wind Palace) at the midline below the occipital bump; <strong>Yifeng</strong> (TE
        17, Wind Screen) behind the ear; <strong>Fengmen</strong> (BL 12, Wind Gate) beside the spine. A finger’s width above Fengfu,
        on the bump itself, is <strong>Naohu</strong> (GV 17), the Brain’s Door. Needling and acupressure act at points; cupping and gua sha
        act on the plane — lifting and shearing it until “sha” rises.
      </p>
      <p>
        Chinese research has also documented, for decades, <em>propagated sensation along the channel</em>: stimulating a point produces warmth
        or tingling that travels along the meridian at roughly one to ten centimetres a second, halted by pressure or cold — the profile, the
        essay notes, of conducted vasodilation climbing a tree.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="daoist">
      <span class="t-glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">玉枕</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">Daoist internal alchemy</p>
      <h2>The orbit and its three gates</h2>
      <p>
        In <em>neidan</em>, internal alchemy, the practitioner circulates the breath-energy in the <em>microcosmic orbit</em>: up the Governor
        vessel along the spine and over the head, down the Conception vessel in front. Three gates block the way up the back —
        <strong>Weilü</strong> at the tailbone, <strong>Jiaji</strong> between the shoulder blades, and <strong>Yuzhen</strong>, the Jade Pillow, at
        the occiput. The third is described as the hardest to open; its opening is described as light filling the head. Three dantian — below
        the navel, at the heart, between the brows — are the fields in which the work is done.
      </p>
      <p>This essay’s gate at the ridge is the Jade Pillow.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="india">
      <span class="t-glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">नाडी</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">Yoga and tantra</p>
      <h2>Nāḍīs, cakras, and the three knots</h2>
      <p>
        The yogic body is a network of <em>nāḍīs</em> — seventy-two thousand of them in the Haṭha Yoga Pradīpikā, three hundred and fifty
        thousand in the Śiva Saṃhitā — of which three matter most: <em>suṣumṇā</em> in the centre, <em>iḍā</em> and <em>piṅgalā</em> winding
        on either side. Along the central channel sit the <em>cakras</em>, and at three of them, knots: the <strong>granthis</strong>. The knot
        of Brahmā low in the body, the knot of Viṣṇu at the heart, the knot of Rudra between the brows. Breath and attention pierce them in
        sequence, and each piercing is described as a stage.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the kuṇḍalinī literature the energy is a serpent coiled at the base of the spine, rising through the knots to the crown. The
        rising is described as heat, current and involuntary movement — the <em>kriyās</em> — and sometimes as a crisis that takes years to
        settle. The number is a curiosity worth holding lightly: seventy-two thousand channels; about a hundred thousand small perforators.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="tibet">
      <span class="t-glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">རྩ་རླུང་</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">Tibet · tsa lung and Dzogchen</p>
      <h2>Channels, winds, drops — and knots</h2>
      <p>
        Tibetan practice works with <em>tsa</em> (channels), <em>lung</em> (winds) and <em>tigle</em> (drops). A central channel runs from the
        crown to below the navel; right and left channels run beside it and, at the channel-wheels, coil around it, forming
        <strong>channel-knots</strong> — <em>rtsa mdud</em>. Those at the heart are the tightest. The Six Yogas and the tsa lung exercises
        untie them by working the winds with breath, held and released; the traditions warn against untying them too fast.
      </p>
      <p>
        Dzogchen adds a channel of its own, the <em>kati</em> — a crystal tube connecting the heart to the eyes — and makes the tradition’s most
        extreme claim about the body at the end: the <strong>rainbow body</strong>, <em>’ja’ lus</em>, in which the body of a realised
        practitioner is said to shrink at death and dissolve into light, leaving hair and nails. The essay’s own endpoint is humbler and
        measurable: a body with all its perforators and none of its knots.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="buddhist">
      <span class="t-glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">龍</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">Buddhist practice and iconography</p>
      <h2>Saṅkhāras, and the serpent over the head</h2>
      <p>
        In the Vipassana taught by S. N. Goenka, gross, solidified bodily sensations are <em>saṅkhāras</em> — congealed reactions — that
        dissolve under equanimous attention and breath. The original essay noticed that this is, nearly word for word, the foam-rolling
        protocol, with attention as the probe.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the awakening, the Udāna says, a great storm came; the nāga king <strong>Mucalinda</strong> coiled his body around the seated
        Buddha and spread his hood above his head for seven days. In the iconography the hood is seven-headed, a canopy over the crown.
        Beneath it sits the <em>uṣṇīṣa</em>, the rise at the top of the Buddha’s head. It is imagery, not evidence — but it rhymes with one of
        the strangest reports in the essay: a layer at the back of the head that peels, and spreads forward over the face.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="kriya">
      <span class="t-glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">延髄</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">The medulla</p>
      <h2>Where the life-force enters</h2>
      <p>
        The Kriya lineage places the entry of the life-force at the medulla, at the base of the skull — the same place accounts of awakening,
        across traditions, locate the decisive release: low, at the back, felt inside the head. The anatomy there is a ring of attachments
        around the foramen magnum — nuchal ligament behind, pharyngeal raphe in front, the myodural bridge between — which is why the essay
        calls it the master tether, and why it is also the one place no one should press hard.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="japan">
      <span class="t-glyph cjk" aria-hidden="true">凝り</span>
      <p class="eyebrow">Japan</p>
      <h2>Kori</h2>
      <p>
        Japanese has an everyday word for a knot: <em>kori</em> (凝り), congealing — and <em>katakori</em>, shoulder stiffness, is among the most
        commonly reported complaints in the country. Shiatsu presses <em>tsubo</em>, points; Zen training gathers the breath into the
        <em>hara</em>, below the navel.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="table" class="wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">Proposed correspondences</p>
      <h2>Read beside the anatomy</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Traditional structure</th><th>Tradition</th><th>Proposed correlate (the essay)</th><th>Status</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {CORRESPONDENCES.map((r) => (
              <tr>{r.map((c) => <td>{c}</td>)}</tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">
        The essay lists the blind coding of these correspondences as a project (VASO-R006): that the classical points are perforators, the
        sinew-channel knots are attachment lines, and the channels are the trees and planes between them.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .trad {
    max-width: 68rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--gutter);
  }
  .trad section {
    position: relative;
    max-width: 42rem;
    padding: 4.5rem 0 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hair);
    scroll-margin-top: 5rem;
  }
  .trad section:first-child {
    border-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .trad section.wide {
    max-width: none;
  }
  .trad h2 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 3.6vw, 2.9rem);
    margin: 0.8rem 0 1.6rem;
  }
  .trad p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .trad strong,
  .trad em {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .trad strong {
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .t-glyph {
    position: absolute;
    right: -24rem;
    top: 4.2rem;
    font-size: 3rem;
    color: var(--ink-4);
    opacity: 0.6;
    writing-mode: vertical-rl;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 50rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.2rem 0.9rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hair);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  td:first-child {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }
  .fine {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--ink-3) !important;
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
    max-width: 44rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
    .t-glyph {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
